{"timestamp":"2026-08-10T15:29:03.417Z","ordinal":20,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_019fec4a-a7b9-7c50-8ee2-b50f475f38c5","call_id":"call_bMSIreh6D3tBCUeEPYxflTAQ","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 102715)\nTotal output lines: 2028\n\n---\nname: stop-first\ndescription: Porte d'intelligence AVANT toute tâche, tous domaines (code, média, admin, recherche, apprentissage, business) — vérifier en une sonde bon marché si la tâche est nécessaire, STOPPER si elle ne l'est pas, et calibrer la taille de la réponse au besoin réel (une ligne si une ligne suffit). L'économie de tokens et de moyens EST l'intelligence. Utiliser proactivement avant tout pipeline lourd, toute tâche batch, tout achat de calcul/tokens ; et quand Matthieu dit \"sois intelligent\", \"économe\", \"stop si inutile\", \"token logic\", \"est-ce que ça vaut le coup\". Complément inverse de think-of-everything : lui vérifie TOUT avant d'agir sur du risqué, stop-first vérifie s'il faut agir DU TOUT.\n---\n\n# stop-first\n\nLa tâche la plus rapide, la moins chère et la plus sûre est celle qu'on ne fait pas. Prouver l'inutilité coûte quelques secondes ; faire l'inutile coûte des minutes, des tokens et du risque.\n\n## Protocole (dans cet ordre, avant tout travail)\n\n1. **Sonde bon marché d'abord.** UNE commande de lecture (< 5 s) capable de prouver que la tâche est inutile,\n   avant d'engager quoi que ce soit de lourd. L'outil le moins cher qui prouve : ffprobe avant de décoder,\n   magic bytes avant de traiter, existsSync avant d'uploader, grep avant de lire le fichier entier, ls avant du,\n   1 URL par commit plutôt que N.\n\n2. **Conditions de stop explicites.** Résultat déjà présent · entrée invalide/incomplète · objectif déjà atteint ·\n   demande déjà satisfaite par un travail antérieur · prérequis manquant qui rend tout le reste caduc.\n   Une condition vraie → **STOP + une ligne** qui dit quoi et pourquoi. Ne pas \"faire quand même pour bien faire\".\n\n3. **Réponse calibrée au besoin.** Question fermée = réponse fermée. Si ça tient en 1 ligne, 1 ligne.\n   Pas de récap, pas de tableau, pas de menu quand il n'y a pas d'enjeu. Le silence fait partie de la réponse.\n\n4. **Escalade seulement sur preuve.** Le pipeline lourd ne démarre que si la sonde a prouvé qu'il est nécessaire —\n   et à la plus petite échelle qui suffit (1 fichier test avant le batch, 1 agent avant la flotte).\n   Subagents/workflows uniquement si le volume ferait exploser le contexte ou si le wall time est critique.\n\n5. **Stopper en cours vaut aussi.** Un fait découvert en route qui invalide la suite → arrêter là, dire pourquoi,\n   ne pas finir par inertie.\n\n## Cas réels (preuves du 2026-07-13)\n\n- 6 épisodes « à traduire + remux » : ffprobe 2 s → 4 avaient déjà le SRT FR, 2 étaient des torrents incomplets\n  (magic EBML absent) → 0 traduction lancée au lieu de 5, tâche entière résolue par la sonde.\n- Upload 168 images : skip local par existence du manifeste dans le clone → relance = 0 réseau.\n\n## Ce que ce skill n'est PAS\n\n- Pas une excuse pour sous-livrer : si la tâche est nécessaire, elle est faite à fond (10/10).\n- Pas un contournement des règles dures (append-only, Git-only >=100, vérifier avant nettoyer) : elles s'appliquent après la porte.\n- Pour le risqué/irréversible, c'est `think-of-everything` qui prend le relais (premortem exhaustif).\n\n---\nname: think-of-everything\ndescription: Run a systematic impact and premortem pass on any decision or change in any domain — code, architecture, product, business, life/admin, travel, purchases, health, learning, art/3D/creative. Reads the request four ways (literal, inverse, adjacent, reframed), verifies the real facts instead of guessing from memory (grep the codebase, read the actual documents and numbers, search official sources), checks a 12-axis impact surface (existing state, conflicts, limits and deadlines, security/legal, partial failure, backward compatibility, scale, recurring cost, observability, edge cases, checks, reversibility), runs a domain-specific premortem, then delivers at most 5 ranked risks with exact proof and a concrete verification each, ending in a GO / NO-GO verdict at paid-specialist precision, never generic. Use whenever the user says \"pense à tout\", \"think of everything\", \"qu'est-ce que j'ai oublié\", \"what am I missing\", asks what a change or decision might break, or wants a premortem, dependency check, or impact analysis. Also trigger proactively, without the phrase, before shipping anything touching shared state, storage or quotas, concurrency, auth or permissions, payments, a public API/route — or any decision that is expensive, irreversible, or deadline-bound.\n---\n\n# Think of Everything (Pense à Tout)\n\nSurface the FEW risks that matter on ANY decision — code, business, life, art — each proven against the real facts, each with a concrete check, ending in a decision. 40 generic checklist items is a failure; 5 proven, ranked risks is a success. This pass answers the question asked, its opposite, and the ones nobody asked, then verifies against reality instead of memory.\n\n**Specialist rule (anti-generic), applies to every output line:** write at the level of the expert who gets paid to review exactly this — immigration lawyer, senior rigger, tax advisor, DB architect. Every kept risk must contain at least one exact value from the real context (number, date, name, path, price, spec) verified this session. A risk that could be pasted unchanged into someone else's situation is generic: sharpen it or cut it.\n\n## Step 0: Classify and scale (two lines)\n\n- Domain + the specialist to embody + type: (a) action to take, (b) existing thing to audit, (c) pure decision, nothing built yet.\n- For (b), the source of truth is the actual artifact (`git diff`, the contract, the scene file), never its description.\n- Trivial, cheap and reversible case: say so, jump to Step 4 + verdict, 5 output lines max. The full pass is for anything expensive, irreversible, deadline-bound, or touching shared state, money, auth, public APIs.\n\n## Step 1: Read the request four ways (one line each)\n\n1. **Literal**: restate exactly what was asked.\n2. **Inverse**: what if the premise is false? What single condition breaks this? And what does NOT doing it cost?\n3. **Adjacent**: the 2-3 questions the specialist would ask next, unprompted. Parallel questions, not follow-ups.\n4. **Reframed**: the clearest version of this request. If it differs from the literal one, answer that too.\n\n## Step 2: Verify the real facts (never from memory)\n\n**Evidence rule, the core of this skill: every claim cites its proof — file:line, URL of an official source, a measured value, or a posed calculation — found THIS SESSION, or carries the label [non vérifié]. No exceptions.**\n\nSources by domain:\n\n- **Code**: `rg -n \"<symbol|route|table|key>\"` across the whole repo — every call site, not just the edited file. Hyrum's law: who depends on the CURRENT undocumented behavior (output format, ordering, timing, error text)? Does the new code re-read data written by the old code?\n- **Business / life / admin**: read the actual documents (contracts, statements, notes, Memory files); web-search official sources for external facts (rules, prices, deadlines, opening days); compute the real numbers and dates — count, never estimate.\n- **Physical / creative (3D, drawing, video, print)**: open the actual source files (scene, layers, footage); check the official specs (software docs, platform limits, physical medium constraints); measure, don't eyeball.\n- **People**: who else is affected, who must agree, whose workflow breaks if this changes?\n\nIf the facts can't be reached, label the whole map \"non vérifié / spéculatif\". Never present a guess as a finding.\n\n## Step 3: Impact surface (12 universal axes, one killer question each)\n\n1. **Existing state**: does what already exists (data, commitments, acquired rights) stay valid? Partial overwrite possible?\n2. **Conflicts / concurrency**: two actors or processes on the same resource at once — then what?\n3. **Limits & deadlines**: which cap (budget, size, rate, date, capacity) gets hit first, and what happens at the boundary?\n4. **Security / legal**: who can trigger or abuse this? Compliant with law, terms, licenses?\n5. **Partial failure**: stopped halfway — what state remains, is it recoverable?\n6. **Backward compatibility**: old data, clients, habits, commitments still work?\n7. **Scale & durability**: still fine at 100x the volume, or repeated weekly for a year?\n8. **Recurring cost**: money, time, energy that scales with usage?\n9. **Observability**: if it fails silently, how and when do you find out?\n10. **Edge cases**: empty/first-run, oversized, duplicate, offline; holiday, sick day, exception case.\n11. **Checks**: a test or verification that catches the regression BEFORE real impact?\n12. **Reversibility**: one action to undo? Does what got engaged meanwhile (money sent, word given, data written) survive?\n\nKeep only the applicable axes, expressed in the domain's own units and vocabulary. Dismiss the rest in one line (\"N/A: ...\"), never pad.\n\n## Step 4: Premortem (narrative, not checklist)\n\n\"Three weeks later it failed, and it was THIS decision. Write the most plausible incident, then the second.\" Use THIS domain's high-frequency classics — code: off-by-one, empty/first-run state, stale cache, non-idempotent retry, timezone/encoding, two concurrent writers; admin/life: missed deadline, missing document, misread eligibility rule, transfer delay, closed office; business: nobody pays, no distribution channel; 3D/art/print: wrong units or scale, topology breaks at rig, export color shift, asset license.\n\n## Step 5: Rank, cut, decide\n\n- Rank each risk: severity (cosmetic → irreversible loss) x likelihood. Irreversible bumps severity one level.\n- Keep the top 5 maximum. Dismissed risks get one summary line total.\n- Every kept risk carries its **proof** (per Step 2) and **one concrete check** to confirm or kill it: a command, a test, a call, a calculation, a measurement.\n\n## Output format (compact, French by default on this account)\n\n```\nDécision/changement : 1 à 3 lignes.\nAngles morts (4 lectures) : 1 ligne chacune.\n| # | Risque | Preuve | Sév x Prob | Vérification |\nÉcartés : 1 ligne.\nQuestions ouvertes : numérotées, chacune avec un défaut recommandé (accord par silence).\nVerdict : GO / GO avec corrections (listées) / NO-GO + correction minimale.\n```\n\n## Stop conditions and anti-goals\n\n- **ONE pass.** Re-run only if the facts changed afterwards, and only on the delta. Never apply this skill to its own output.\n- It audits ONE stated decision or change. It does not generate unsolicited master plans.\n- Not a substitute for tests, CI, or licensed professionals on high-stakes legal, medical or financial moves: it catches known failure classes, not novel ones. Say that plainly instead of implying completeness.\n\nWarning: truncated output (original token count: 194627)\nTotal output lines: 3428\n\n    3427  778479 PROGRESS.md\n# PROGRESS — Memory Repository\n\nJournal chronologique append only. Plus ancienne entrée en haut, plus récente en bas. Voir `CLAUDE.md` (section JOURNAL DE PROGRESSION CHRONOLOGIQUE) pour les règles.\n\n---\n\n2026-05-19 00:46\n- Fait : Initialisation du journal PROGRESS.md à la racine du repo Memory. Ajout de deux règles dans `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (global, tous projets) et dans `CLAUDE.md` du repo Memory : (1) Clarté pédagogique (limpide + synthétique + simple ET complexe, compréhensible sales/dev/enfant 8 ans/mamie 85 ans), (2) Journal de progression chronologique (PROGRESS.md append only à la racine de chaque projet, lecture obligatoire en début de session, écriture automatique sans demander).\