{"timestamp":"2026-08-10T15:29:47.457Z","ordinal":42,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_019fec4b-53c1-7993-a811-d89126ba66d6","call_id":"call_SlNNUaQ41qVUHB0miCGsfTrM","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.7 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Back up your Mac with Time Machine - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/en-us/104984)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last month; # Back up your Mac with Time Machine ...      * Your Mac might ask if you want to erase the storage device so that it can be used for Time Machine. ... To change the backup frequency in macOS Ventura or later, choose Open Time Machine Settings from the Time Machine menu Image: No alt supplied for Image.\n\n# Back up your Mac with Time Machine\n\nIf you have a USB drive or other external storage device, you can use Time Machine to automatically back up your files, including apps, music, photos, email, and documents.\n\n  * Connect a storage device to your Mac\n\n  * Set up the storage device as your backup disk\n\n  * Make a backup\n\n  * Learn about backup frequency and duration\n\n  * Restore your Mac from a backup\n\n## Connect a storage device to your Mac\n\nConnect an external storage device, such as a USB drive or Thunderbolt drive, to the appropriate port on your Mac. Identify the ports on your Mac.\n\n  * Use a storage device with at least twice the storage capacity of your Mac. For example, if your Mac has 1TB of storage, your backup disk should ideally have at least 2TB of storage. If Time Machine needs more storage space, your Mac will let you know.\n\n  * Use your Time Machine backup disk only for Time Machine backups, not for storage of other files.*\n\n## Set up the storage device as your backup disk\n\nLearn the setup steps in your version of macOS, or follow these steps in macOS Ventura 13 or later:\n\n  1. From the Apple menu  in the corner of your screen, choose System Settings.\n\n  2. Click General in the sidebar, then click Time Machine on the right.\n\n  3. Click Add Backup Disk, or click the Add button (+).\n\n  4. Select your connected storage device and click Set Up Disk. During setup:\n\n     * Your Mac might ask if you want to erase the storage device so that it can be used for Time Machine. Either erase it or select a different backup disk or volume.\n\n     * Your Mac might ask whether this computer should claim existing backups created on a different computer. Claim the backups if you want them to become part of the backups for this Mac.\n\n     * If you turn on Encrypt Backup to help secure your backup from unauthorized access, you’re asked to create a backup password. You will need it if you later attempt to restore files from the backup.\n\nAfter setup, your Mac immediately begins using the connected storage device to make backups.\n\n## Make a backup\n\nTo back up now, choose Back Up Now from the Time Machine menuImage: No alt supplied for Image in the menu bar. Or wait for the next automatic backup.\n\nUse the same menu to stop or skip a backup or check its progress. The top of the menu shows how much of the current backup is done, or the date and time of the latest backup:\n\nImage: Time Machine menu showing a backup in progress.\n\nBackup is in progress\n\nImage: Time Machine menu showing details about the latest backup.\n\nBackup is done\n\nIf you don't see this menu, make sure that Control Center settings (macOS Ventura or later) or Desktop & Menu Bar preferences (macOS Monterey or earlier) are set to show Time Machine in the menu bar.\n\n## Learn about backup frequency and duration\n\nTime Machine automatically makes hourly backups for the past 24 hours, daily backups for the past month, and weekly backups for all previous months. The oldest backups are deleted when your backup disk is full.\n\nTo change the backup frequency in macOS Ventura or later, choose Open Time Machine Settings from the Time Machine menuImage: No alt supplied for Image. Click Options, then choose a setting from the “Back up frequency” menu.\n\nThe first backup might take longer than you expect, but you can continue using your Mac while a backup is underway. Time Machine backs up only the files that changed since the previous backup, so future backups will be faster.\n\nIf you disconnect your backup disk from your Mac, Time Machine will automatically resume its backup schedule when you reconnect it to your Mac. Before disconnecting your backup disk, either eject the backup disk or shut down your Mac.\n\n## Restore your Mac from a backup\n\nYou can use your Time Machine backup to restore any or all of your files to the same Mac or a different Mac. Learn how to restore your Mac from a Time Machine backup.\n\n* Advanced users: If you add an APFS volume to the external storage device, you can use one volume for Time Machine backups and the other for storage of other files. The Time Machine volume should be twice the storage capacity of your Mac.\n\nInformation about products not manufactured by Apple, or independent websites not controlled or tested by Apple, is provided without recommendation or endorsement. Apple assumes no responsibility with regard to the selection, performance, or use of third-party websites or products. Apple makes no representations regarding third-party website accuracy or reliability. Contact the vendor for additional information.\n\nPublished Date: May 22, 2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIf you can't back up or restore your Mac using Time Machine  - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/en-us/102220)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # If you can't back up or restore your Mac using Time Machine ...   * If Time Machine reports that the backup is delayed or could not be completed because some files were unavailable, complete a manual backup while your Mac is unlocked, or wait for Time Machine to try again during future automatic backups. ...   * If you’re attempting to restore from a backup that was created in macOS Monterey 12 or later, make sure that your Mac is using macOS Big Sur 11 or later.\n\n# If you can't back up or restore your Mac using Time Machine\n\nIf Time Machine can't use your backup disk to back up or restore your files, try these solutions.\n\n## How to back up or restore\n\n  * How to back up your Mac with Time Machine\n\n  * How to restore your Mac from a Time Machine backup\n\n## If you can't back up or restore\n\n  * Learn what to do if your Mac doesn’t start up all the way.\n\n  * Make sure that your backup disk is supported by Time Machine.\n\n  * Check storage space.\n\n    * If Time Machine says that your backup disk doesn't have enough storage space or is nearly full, use a backup disk that has at least twice the storage capacity of your Mac. For example, if your Mac has 1 TB of storage, your backup disk should ideally have at least 2 TB of storage. If necessary, you can choose items to exclude from your backup.\n\n    * Check the storage space on your Mac as well. If your startup disk is nearly full, Time Machine might not be able to save the temporary data needed to complete a backup.   * If Time Machine reports that the backup is delayed or could not be completed because some files were unavailable, complete a manual backup while your Mac is unlocked, or wait for Time Machine to try again during future automatic backups. (Certain protected files might require that your Mac be unlocked before Time Machine can access them.)\n\n  * Check for VPN software or other third-party security software, which can affect the connection to your backup disk.\n\n  * Use Disk Utility to repair your backup disk.\n\n  * Make sure that the firmware of your backup disk is up to date. Check with the manufacturer of the backup disk for details.\n\n  * If you’re attempting to restore from a backup that was created in macOS Monterey 12 or later, make sure that your Mac is using macOS Big Sur 11 or later.\n\n  * If no other solutions work, start over: Erase your backup disk, then use Time Machine to select it as your backup disk. Or try a different backup disk.\n\nLearn what to do if Time Machine is working but takes a long time to back up.\n\n## Need more help?\n\nTell us more about what's happening, and we’ll suggest what you can do next.\n\nGet suggestions\n\nPublished Date: July 06, 2026--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReinstall macOS – Apple Support (UK) (https://support.apple.com/en-gb/guide/mac-help/mchlp1599/26/mac/26)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago;        * Reinstall your computer’s original version of macOS (including available updates): Option-Shift-Command-R. ... On a Mac with Apple silicon or an Intel-based Mac with the Apple T2 Security Chip, use Erase Assistant to reset your Mac to factory settings before you trade it in or sell it. ... See also Intro to macOS Recovery Recover all your files from a Time Machine backup Restore items backed up with Time Machine on Mac Software Update settings on Mac Apple Support article: What to do before you sell, give away, trade in or recycle your Mac\n\n# Reinstall macOS\n\nYou can use macOS Recovery, the built-in recovery system on your Mac, to reinstall macOS. macOS Recovery keeps your files and user settings intact when reinstalling.\n\nImportant: Your computer must be connected to the internet to reinstall macOS.\n\n  1. Start up your computer in macOS Recovery:\n\n     * On a Mac with Apple silicon: Choose Apple menu Image > Shut Down, press and hold the power button on your Mac until the system volume and the Options button appear, select Options, click Continue, then follow the on-screen instructions.\n\n     * On an Intel-based Mac: Choose Apple menu Image > Restart, then immediately press and hold one of these key combinations, depending on what you want to do:\n\n       * Install the latest version of macOS compatible with your computer: Option-Command-R.\n\n       * Reinstall your computer’s original version of macOS (including available updates): Option-Shift-Command-R.\n\n       * Reinstall your current version of macOS: Command-R.\n\n  2. In the Recovery app window, click Reinstall macOS [name], then click Continue.\n\n  3. Follow the onscreen instructions.\n\n  4. In the settings where you select a volume, select your current macOS volume (in most cases, it’s the only one available).\n\nOn a Mac with Apple silicon or an Intel-based Mac with the Apple T2 Security Chip, use Erase Assistant to reset your Mac to factory settings before you trade it in or sell it. See Erase your Mac.\n\nSee alsoIntro to macOS RecoveryRecover all your files from a Time Machine backupRestore items backed up with Time Machine on MacSoftware Update settings on MacApple Support article: What to do before you sell, give away, trade in or recycle your Mac--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReinstall macOS - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/en-mide/guide/mac-help/mchlp1599/mac)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;        * Reinstall your computer’s original version of macOS (including available updates): Option-Shift-Command-R. ... On a Mac with Apple silicon or an Intel-based Mac with the Apple T2 Security Chip, use Erase Assistant to reset your Mac to factory settings before you trade it in or sell it. ... See also Intro to macOS Recovery Recover all your files from a Time Machine backup Restore items backed up with Time Machine on Mac Software Update settings on Mac Apple Support article: What to do before you sell, give away, trade in, or recycle your Mac\n\nImage\n\n# Reinstall macOS\n\nYou can use macOS Recovery, the built-in recovery system on your Mac, to reinstall macOS. macOS Recovery keeps your files and user settings intact when reinstalling.\n\nImportant: Your computer must be connected to the internet to reinstall macOS.\n\n  1. Start up your computer in macOS Recovery:\n\n     * On a Mac with Apple silicon: Choose Apple menu Image > Shut Down, press and hold the power button on your Mac until the system volume and the Options button appear, select Options, click Continue, then follow the onscreen instructions.\n\n     * On an Intel-based Mac: Choose Apple menu Image > Restart, then immediately press and hold one of these key combinations, depending on what you want to do:\n\n       * Install the latest version of macOS compatible with your computer: Option-Command-R.\n\n       * Reinstall your computer’s original version of macOS (including available updates): Option-Shift-Command-R.\n\n       * Reinstall your current version of macOS: Command-R.\n\n  2. In the Recovery app window, click Reinstall macOS [name], then click Continue.\n\n  3. Follow the onscreen instructions.\n\n  4. In the settings where you select a volume, select your current macOS volume (in most cases, it’s the only one available).\n\nOn a Mac with Apple silicon or an Intel-based Mac with the Apple T2 Security Chip, use Erase Assistant to reset your Mac to factory settings before you trade it in or sell it. See Erase your Mac.\n\nSee alsoIntro to macOS RecoveryRecover all your files from a Time Machine backupRestore items backed up with Time Machine on MacSoftware Update settings on MacApple Support article: What to do before you sell, give away, trade in, or recycle your Mac--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUse macOS Recovery on a Mac with Apple silicon - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/macos-recovery-a-mac-apple-silicon-mchl82829c17/mac)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; You can use the apps in macOS Recovery on a Mac with Apple silicon to repair your computer’s internal storage, reinstall macOS, restore your files from a Time Machine backup, set the security policy for different volumes, transfer files between two Mac computers, start up in safe mode, and more. ... To return to the Recovery app, choose Install macOS [name] > Quit Install macOS. ... If asked, select a volume to recover, then click Next. ... To upgrade from the installed version to the latest version of macOS, go to Apple menu Image > System Settings, click General Image in the sidebar (you may need to scroll down), then click Software Update. ... This mode requires a network connection at software installation time.\n\n# Use macOS Recovery on a Mac with Apple silicon\n\nmacOS Recovery is the built-in recovery system on your Mac.\n\nYou can use the apps in macOS Recovery on a Mac with Apple silicon to repair your computer’s internal storage, reinstall macOS, restore your files from a Time Machine backup, set the security policy for different volumes, transfer files between two Mac computers, start up in safe mode, and more.\n\nReinstalling macOS requires an internet connection. To connect to the internet, you can use a wireless or wired network connection. If you’re trying to connect to Wi-Fi through a captive portal (for example, at a coffee shop, library, or hotel) or an enterprise network, you might not be able to access the internet in macOS Recovery. See Connect to the internet using Wi-Fi.\n\n## Apps available in macOS Recovery on a Mac with Apple silicon\n\nThe following apps are available in macOS Recovery on a Mac with Apple silicon:\n\n  * Recovery: The Recovery app provides access to additional apps in macOS Recovery. See Start up your computer in macOS Recovery, Start up your computer in macOS Recovery and pair Bluetooth input devices, and Quit macOS Recovery.\n\n  * Time Machine System Restore: Restore your data from a Time Machine backup. In the Recovery app, select “Restore from Time Machine,” then click Continue. To return to the Recovery app, choose Time Machine System Restore > Quit Time Machine System Restore. See Use Time Machine to restore your files.\n\n  * Install macOS: Reinstall macOS on your computer. In the Recovery app, select Reinstall macOS [name], then click Continue. To return to the Recovery app, choose Install macOS [name] > Quit Install macOS. See Reinstall macOS.\n\n  * Web Browser: In the Recovery app, select Web Browser, then click Continue. When you go to the browser, this page is displayed. If you’re connected to the internet, you can use the browser to search the web. To set the search engine the browser uses, choose Web Browser > Search Engine, then choose a search engine. ## Use Time Machine to restore your files\n\nIf you previously created a Time Machine backup, you can restore your files from the backup.\n\nImportant: Only use your Time Machine backup to restore information to the computer that is the source of the backup. If you want to transfer information from this Mac to a new computer, quit macOS Recovery, restart your Mac, then use the Migration Assistant app in macOS.\n\n  1. Start up your computer in macOS Recovery.\n\n  2. If your backup is on a Time Capsule, make sure you’re connected to the internet.\n\n  3. In the Recovery app, select “Restore from Time Machine,” then click Continue.\n\n  4. In the Time Machine System Restore app, follow the onscreen instructions.\n\n## Reinstall macOS\n\nUnder some circumstances, you may need to reinstall macOS. You can reinstall macOS while keeping your files and user settings intact.\n\n  1. Start up your computer in macOS Recovery.\n\n  2. Make sure you’re connected to the internet.\n\n  3. In the Recovery app, click Reinstall macOS [name], then click Continue.\n\n  4. Follow the onscreen instructions.\n\nImportant: When you’re asked to select a disk, select your current macOS disk (in most cases, it’s the only one available).\n\n## Erase and reinstall macOS\n\nImportant: Do not follow these steps if you want to get your Mac ready to trade in, sell, or give away. Instead, use Erase Assistant in macOS to reset your Mac to factory settings. Erase Assistant removes all your personal information, Activation Lock, and trusted iCloud status from your Mac. To use Erase Assistant, start up your computer in macOS, choose Apple menu Image > System Settings, click General Image in the sidebar (you may need to scroll down), click Transfer or Reset, click the Erase All Content and Settings button, then follow the onscreen instructions.\n\nIf you erase and reinstall macOS, your user accounts, network settings, and all of the files and folders on your Mac are deleted. Before you erase macOS, attempt to log back in to your system, then back up any files you want to keep by copying them to another storage device. Also, write down your network settings from Network settings to make it easier to get connected again after reinstalling macOS.\n\n  1. Start up your computer in macOS Recovery.\n\n  2. Make sure you’re connected to the internet.\n\n  3. In the Recovery app, click Disk Utility, then click Continue.\n\n  4. In Disk Utility, select the volume you want to erase in the sidebar, then click the Erase button in the toolbar.\n\n  5. Type a name for the volume in the Name field.\n\n  6. Click the Format pop-up menu, choose APFS, then click Erase Volume Group.\n\n  7. When the erase process is complete, click Done, then choose Disk Utility > Quit Disk Utility.\n\n  8. In the Recovery app, click Reinstall macOS [name], click Continue, then follow the onscreen instructions.\n\nNote: When you erase and reinstall macOS, an earlier version of macOS may be installed. To upgrade from the installed version to the latest version of macOS, go to Apple menu Image > System Settings, click General Image in the sidebar (you may need to scroll down), then click Software Update.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to reinstall macOS - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/en-euro/102655)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; After starting up from macOS Recovery, you should see a list of options, including the option to install or reinstall macOS. ... During installation, your Mac might restart and show a progress bar several times, and the screen might be empty for minutes at a time. ... On a Mac with Apple silicon: ...   * If you used Command-R to start up from the local Recovery system, you get the current version of the most recently installed macOS.\n\n# How to reinstall macOS\n\nUse macOS Recovery, your computer’s built-in recovery system, to reinstall the Mac operating system.\n\n  * Reinstall macOS from macOS Recovery\n\n  * Which macOS will be installed?\n\n  * If you can’t reinstall macOS\n\n## Reinstall macOS from macOS Recovery\n\n  1. Back up your Mac, if possible. Reinstalling macOS doesn't remove your apps or personal data, but it’s still a good idea to make a backup first.\n\n  2. Start up from macOS Recovery. The steps to do this depend on the type of Mac you’re using.\n\n  3. After starting up from macOS Recovery, you should see a list of options, including the option to install or reinstall macOS. Select that option, then click Continue and follow the onscreen instructions.\n\nImage: Utilities in macOS Recovery\n  4. Allow installation to complete without putting your Mac to sleep or closing its lid. During installation, your Mac might restart and show a progress bar several times, and the screen might be empty for minutes at a time.\n\n     * If the installer asks to unlock your disk, enter the password that you use to log in to your Mac.\n\n     * If the installer offers the choice between installing on “Macintosh HD” or “Macintosh HD - Data”, choose Macintosh HD.\n\n     * Learn what to do if you can’t reinstall macOS.\n\n  5. After installation, your Mac might start up to a setup assistant. Follow the steps in the setup assistant. However, if you're selling, trading in, or giving away your Mac, quit the setup assistant without completing setup, then click Shut Down. When the new owner starts up the Mac, they can use their own information to complete setup.\n\n## Which macOS will be installed?\n\nOn a Mac with Apple silicon:\n\n  * Recovery installs the current version of the most recently installed macOS.\n\n  * If you installed a macOS upgrade and then used Disk Utility to erase the disk, you might get the macOS that you were using before upgrading.\n\nOn any other Mac:\n\n  * If you used Command-R to start up from the local Recovery system, you get the current version of the most recently installed macOS.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUpdate devices using Apple Configurator for Mac - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/en-euro/guide/apple-configurator-mac/cad789a3f0bd/mac)\nciteturn0search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Update devices using Apple Configurator for Mac ...   * In Apple Configurator Image, select the device in the device window and drag the .ipsw file onto the device, then choose to update or restore the device.\n\n# Update devices using Apple Configurator for Mac\n\nUpdate iPhone, iPad, or Apple TV devices to install the latest apps, operating system version, or both.\n\nNote: Apple Configurator doesn’t support updating a Mac or Apple Vision Pro. See Revive and restore Apple devices.\n\n## Update devices\n\n  1. In Apple Configurator Image, attach all the devices you want to update, then select those devices in the device window.\n\n  2. Click the Update button Image in the toolbar.\n\n  3. Select the type of update you want to perform:\n\n     * Apps only\n\n     * Latest operating system version\n\n     * Latest operating system version and apps\n\n## Update beta software\n\n  * In Apple Configurator Image, install beta software on a device, select the device in the device window, and drag the .ipsw file onto the device.\n\nFor more information about beta operating system versions of iOS, iPadOS, and tvOS, see AppleSeed for IT.\n\n## Update or restore iPhone, iPad, or Apple TV devices\n\n  * In Apple Configurator Image, select the device in the device window and drag the .ipsw file onto the device, then choose to update or restore the device.\n\nNote: Because of dependency on architecture and system changes to any current version of an Apple operating system, if you restore to an older version of software, Apple Configurator notifies you that the operating system may not function properly on the device.\n\n## Add the update or restore function to a Blueprint\n\n  * In Apple Configurator Image, select the Blueprint in the device window and drag the .ipsw file onto the Blueprint. When you’re ready to apply the Blueprint, the update or restore action takes place.\n\nSee alsoOrganize devices in Apple Configurator for MacUse Blueprints in Apple Configurator for Mac--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRecover all your files from a Time Machine backup - Apple Support (IE) (https://support.apple.com/en-ie/guide/mac-help/-mh15638/mac)\nciteturn0search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; After macOS finishes installing new system files, Migration Assistant asks if you want to transfer information from another Mac or a Time Machine backup. ... See also Recover files using Time Machine and Spotlight on Mac Restore items backed up with Time Machine on Mac If your new Mac inherits your backup history View and restore past versions of documents on Mac Apple Support article: How to reinstall macOS\n\nImage\n\n# Recover all your files from a Time Machine backup\n\nIf you use Time Machine to back up your Mac, you can recover your files if your system or startup disk is damaged.\n\nImportant: You must first reinstall macOS on your Mac before you can restore your files using your Time Machine backup.\n\nIf you’re restoring your system because of a problem with your startup disk, repair or replace the disk before following these instructions.\n\nKeep these instructions handy by printing them. Click Image: the Share button, then choose Print.\n\nBefore you begin, make sure your Time Machine backup disk is connected and turned on. If your disk is on a network, make sure your Mac is on the same network.\n\n  1. Start up your computer in macOS Recovery:\n\n     * On a Mac with Apple silicon: Choose Apple menu Image > Shut Down, press and hold the power button until you see “Loading startup options”, click Options, click Continue, then follow the on-screen instructions.\n\n     * On an Intel-based Mac: Choose Apple menu Image > Restart, immediately press and hold Command-R until you see the startup screen, then follow the onscreen instructions.\n\n  2. To reinstall the version of macOS stored on your computer’s built-in recovery disk, including any installed updates, select Reinstall macOS Sonoma in the Recovery window, then click Continue.\n\nSee Reinstall macOS.\n\n  3. After macOS finishes installing new system files, Migration Assistant asks if you want to transfer information from another Mac or a Time Machine backup. Select Transfer from a Time Machine Backup, then click Continue.\n\nIf necessary, enter the name and password you use to connect to your backup disk. You may also have to enter the password you used to encrypt the backup.\n\n  4. Select the date and time of the backup you want to restore, then follow the onscreen instructions.\n\nAfter you restore your system, Time Machine may perform a full backup at the next scheduled backup time. This is normal. Time Machine resumes incremental backups after the full backup is completed.\n\nSee alsoRecover files using Time Machine and Spotlight on MacRestore items backed up with Time Machine on MacIf your new Mac inherits your backup historyView and restore past versions of documents on MacApple Support article: How to reinstall macOS--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBack up your Mac with Time Machine - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/en-mide/104984)\nciteturn0search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; # Back up your Mac with Time Machine ... From the Apple menu  in the corner of your screen, choose System Settings. ...      * Your Mac might ask if you want to erase the storage device so that it can be used for Time Machine. ... To change the backup frequency in macOS Ventura or later, choose Open Time Machine Settings from the Time Machine menu Image: No alt supplied for Image.\nThe oldest backups are deleted when your backup disk is full.\n\nTo change the backup frequency in macOS Ventura or later, choose Open Time Machine Settings from the Time Machine menuImage: No alt supplied for Image. Click Options, then choose a setting from the “Back up frequency” menu.\n\nThe first backup might take longer than you expect, but you can continue using your Mac while a backup is underway. Time Machine backs up only the files that changed since the previous backup, so future backups will be faster.\n\nIf you disconnect your backup disk from your Mac, Time Machine will automatically resume its backup schedule when you reconnect it to your Mac. Before disconnecting your backup disk, either eject the backup disk or shut down your Mac.\n\n## Restore your Mac from a backup\n\nYou can use your Time Machine backup to restore any or all of your files to the same Mac or a different Mac. Learn how to restore your Mac from a Time Machine backup.\n\n## Learn more\n\n  * If you can't make a backup\n\n  * If you want to exclude or stop excluding items from a Time Machine backup\n\n  * If you want to back up to multiple storage devices\n\n  * Other ways to back up your Mac--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRevive an iPhone, iPad, or Apple TV in Apple Configurator for Mac - Apple Support (CA) (https://support.apple.com/en-ca/guide/apple-configurator-mac/cad367dc4593/mac)\nciteturn0search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; See also Back up and restore iPhone and iPad devices in Apple Configurator for Mac Apple Support article: How to revive or restore Mac firmware\n\n# Revive an iPhone, iPad, or Apple TV in Apple Configurator for Mac\n\nIf an iPhone, iPad, or Apple TV (Wi-Fi + Ethernet only) is partially configured, if information about the device (such as the supervision identity) is missing, or if the device is stuck in recovery mode, you can attempt to revive the device to recover any data on it.\n\n  1. In Apple Configurator Image, select one or more devices.\n\n  2. Do one of the following:\n\n     * Choose Actions > Advanced > Revive Device.\n\n     * Control-click the selected devices, and choose Advanced > Revive Device.\n\nSee alsoBack up and restore iPhone and iPad devices in Apple Configurator for MacApple Support article: How to revive or restore Mac firmware\n\nPrevious Set Single App Mode Next Restore an Apple Vision Pro--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUse Disk Utility to erase a Mac with Apple silicon – Apple Support (UK) (https://support.apple.com/en-gb/102506)\nciteturn0search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last week; # Use Disk Utility to erase a Mac with Apple silicon ...   * When you reinstall macOS after erasing your Mac, if you previously installed a macOS upgrade, you may get the macOS that was installed before you upgraded.Or if you installed a beta (pre-release) version of macOS, you’ll get the previous release version of macOS.\n\n# Use Disk Utility to erase a Mac with Apple silicon\n\nUse Disk Utility to erase (format) the built-in startup disk of your Mac. You can then reinstall macOS to enable your Mac to start up from that disk again.\n\nThese steps only apply to Mac computers with Apple silicon. If you’re not using a Mac with Apple silicon, follow the steps to erase an Intel-based Mac.\n\n## Before erasing your Mac\n\n  * If possible, install the latest macOS updates and make a backup of any files you want to keep. Erasing your Mac permanently will delete the files on it.\n\n  * When you reinstall macOS after erasing your Mac, if you previously installed a macOS upgrade, you may get the macOS that was installed before you upgraded. Or if you installed a beta (pre-release) version of macOS, you’ll get the previous release version of macOS. You can reinstall any macOS updates or upgrades afterwards.\n\n  * If you’re erasing your Mac because you’re relinquishing ownership of it, find out what to do before you sell, give away, trade in or recycle your Mac.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTransfer to a new Mac with Migration Assistant - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/en-us/102613)\nciteturn0search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; You can also use Migration Assistant to transfer from a Time Machine backup of your old Mac. ...   * Make sure that macOS is up to date on each Mac. ... Those steps include the option to erase your old Mac and reset it to factory settings.\n\n# Transfer to a new Mac with Migration Assistant\n\nMigration Assistant transfers documents, apps, user accounts, and settings from one Mac to another. It doesn’t delete information from your old Mac or replace the operating system (macOS) on your new Mac.\n\nYou can also use Migration Assistant to transfer from a Time Machine backup of your old Mac. Or transfer from a Windows PC.\n\n## Get ready\n\n  * Make sure that macOS is up to date on each Mac. It’s not necessary for each Mac to be using the same version of macOS.\n\n  * To transfer wirelessly, make sure that both computers have Wi-FiImage turned on and are near each other. If either Mac is using OS X El Capitan or earlier, or you’re not sure, also connect them to the same Wi-Fi network.\n\n  * If antivirus, firewall, or VPN software is in use on either Mac, turn off that software until you're done.\n\n## Use Migration Assistant\n\nMigration Assistant is included with your Mac—both as an app and as part of the setup assistant that opens when you first start up a new Mac.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nApple Accessibility Conformance Report (https://support.apple.com/content/dam/edam/applecare/images/en_US/otherassets/accessibility/vpat_imac_24_inch_2024.pdf)\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; ICT that transmits or converts information or communication shall not remove non-proprietary information provided for accessibility or shall restore it upon delivery. ... More information is available at https://www.apple.com/accessibility/ mac/vision/\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nApple Accessibility Conformance Report (https://support.apple.com/content/dam/edam/applecare/images/en_US/otherassets/accessibility/vpat_macbookpro_16_2023.pdf)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; More information is available at https://www.apple.com/accessibility/ mac/vision/ ... ICT that transmits or converts information or communication shall not remove non-proprietary information provided for accessibility or shall restore it upon delivery.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nApple Accessibility Conformance Report (https://support.apple.com/content/dam/edam/applecare/images/en_US/otherassets/accessibility/vpat_macbookair_m2_2022.pdf)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; ICT that transmits or converts information or communication shall not remove non-proprietary information provided for accessibility or shall restore it upon delivery. ... More information on product technical specifications at https:// www.apple.com/macbook-air-m2/specs/\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nApple Accessibility Conformance Report (https://