n the Disk Utility toolbar.\n\n  6. Click Delete. If the volume is part of a volume group, delete the volume group when prompted.\n\n## Need more help?\n\nTell us more about what’s happening and we’ll suggest what you can do next.\n\nGet suggestions\n\nPublished Date: May 11, 2026--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUsar mais de uma versão do macOS em um Mac - Suporte da Apple (BR) (https://support.apple.com/pt-br/118282)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last week; ﻿ Usar mais de uma versão do macOS em um Mac - Suporte da Apple (BR) ... Esta configuração de “boot duplo” é uma maneira conveniente de experimentar uma versão posterior do macOS, mantendo temporariamente o macOS atualmente instalado para softwares mais antigos que ainda precisam dele. ... ## Preparar um novo volume APFS para o novo macOS\n﻿ Usar mais de uma versão do macOS em um Mac - Suporte da Apple (BR)\n\n# Usar mais de uma versão do macOS em um Mac\n\nSe você instalar um macOS compatível em um volume de armazenamento interno ou externo adicional, o Mac poderá inicializar a partir de qualquer um dos volumes.\n\n## Antes de começar\n\nEsta configuração de “boot duplo” é uma maneira conveniente de experimentar uma versão posterior do macOS, mantendo temporariamente o macOS atualmente instalado para softwares mais antigos que ainda precisam dele.\n\nImportante: essa não é uma maneira de longo prazo de continuar usando um macOS anterior após a atualização. Versões posteriores do macOS podem instalar alterações projetadas para manter o Mac seguro, e essas alterações podem afetar a capacidade do computador de continuar usando uma versão significativamente anterior.\n\n## Preparar um novo volume APFS para o novo macOS\n\nSe você tiver espaço de armazenamento livre suficiente, poderá instalar o novo macOS no disco de inicialização integrado sem remover o macOS já instalado. Ou você pode instalar o macOS em um dispositivo de armazenamento externo.\n\n### Se estiver instalando no disco de inicialização integrado\n\nSe você quiser instalar o novo macOS no disco de inicialização integrado, crie um novo volume APFS para contê-lo.\n\n  1. O Mac deve estar executando o macOS High Sierra ou posterior. Versões anteriores do macOS não são compatíveis com o APFS.\n\n  2. Abra o Utilitário de Disco, que fica na pasta Utilitários, dentro da pasta Aplicativos.\n\n  3. Na barra lateral, selecione o volume APFS existente, como Macintosh HD.\n\n  4. No menu Editar na barra de menus, selecione Adicionar Volume APFS. Ou clique no botão adicionar volume (+) na barra de ferramentas do Utilitário de Disco. Essas opções estão disponíveis apenas quando um volume com formato APFS for selecionado.\n\n  5. Digite um nome para o novo volume e clique em Adicionar.\n\n  6. Quando terminar, encerre o Utilitário de Disco.\n\nImage: Caixa de diálogo \"Adicionar Volume APFS\" no Utilitário de Disco, utilizada para nomear e formatar um novo volume APFS para instalar outro macOS.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmacOS installation - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/guide/platform-support/macos-installation-sup3c682c604/web)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; However, a bootable installer can be useful when those or other macOS installation methods are unsuccessful, or when you want to install macOS on multiple computers without downloading the installer each time. ...   * A compatible version of the macOS installer downloaded from the Mac App Store or the Apple website.For a step-by-step procedure, see Create a bootable installer for macOS.\nAttempts to fix system issues  | Image: YesMinor to moderate issues  | Image: YesSevere to persistent issues\nBest for transferring Mac to new owner  | Image: No  | Image: Yes\n\n## Bootable installer\n\nYou can use an external storage device or other secondary volume as a startup disk from which to install macOS. These steps are primarily for system administrators and other experienced users.\n\nYou don’t need a bootable installer to upgrade macOS or reinstall macOS. However, a bootable installer can be useful when those or other macOS installation methods are unsuccessful, or when you want to install macOS on multiple computers without downloading the installer each time.\n\nHere’s what you need:\n\n  * An external storage device or other secondary volume with at least 16GB of storage.\n\n  * A Mac to download the macOS installer.\n\n  * A compatible version of the macOS installer downloaded from the Mac App Store or the Apple website.\n\nFor a step-by-step procedure, see Create a bootable installer for macOS.\n\nPublished Date: October 22, 2025\n\nSee alsoApple Support article: If an error occurred while updating or installing macOSApple Support article: Connect to the internet with your Mac\n\nPrevious Mac firmware revive and restore Next Mac backups--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRestart your Mac in macOS or Windows - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/en-us/102450)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; After using Boot Camp to install Microsoft Windows on your Intel-based Mac, you can choose whether your Mac starts up in macOS or Windows. ... From the Apple menu , choose System Settings.\n\n# Restart your Mac in macOS or Windows\n\nAfter using Boot Camp to install Microsoft Windows on your Intel-based Mac, you can choose whether your Mac starts up in macOS or Windows.\n\n## Start up in macOS from Windows\n\n  1. From the notification area on the right side of the Windows taskbar, click the caratImage: carat to show hidden icons.\n\n  2. Click the Boot Camp iconImage: No alt supplied for Image.\n\n  3. From the menu shown, choose Restart in macOS. If you haven't installed the latest Boot Camp updates, you might get the message “Could not locate the OS X boot volume.”\n\nImage: boot-camp-win-10-restart\n\nOr follow these steps:\n\n  1. Turn on or restart your Mac. If you're currently in Windows, you can restart by choosing Power from the Start menu. When your Mac begins to restart, press and hold the Option (or Alt) ⌥ key on your keyboard. Release the key when you see a window showing all available startup volumes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUse Windows on your Mac - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/en-euro/guide/mac-help/mh11850/mac)\nciteturn1search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; After installing Windows and the Boot Camp drivers, you can start up your Mac in either Windows or macOS.\n\nImage\n\n# Use Windows on your Mac\n\nWith Boot Camp, you can install and use Windows on your Intel-based Mac.\n\nBoot Camp Assistant helps you set up a Windows partition on your Mac computer’s hard disk and then start the installation of your Windows software.\n\nAfter installing Windows and the Boot Camp drivers, you can start up your Mac in either Windows or macOS.\n\nFor information about using Boot Camp to install Windows, see the Boot Camp Assistant User Guide.\n\nSee alsoWindows keys on a Mac keyboardRight-click on MacWhat’s it called on my Mac?--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdd, delete, or erase APFS volumes in Disk Utility on Mac - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/guide/disk-utility/add-delete-or-erase-apfs-volumes-dskua9e6a110/mac)\nciteturn1search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Apple File System (APFS) allocates disk space on demand. ... On your computer, macOS is installed on a set of volumes known as a volume group. ... Go to the Disk Utility app Image on your Mac.\n\n# Add, delete, or erase APFS volumes in Disk Utility on Mac\n\nApple File System (APFS) allocates disk space on demand. When a single APFS container (partition) has multiple volumes, the container’s free space is shared and can be allocated to any of the individual volumes as needed. Each volume uses only part of the overall container, so the available space is the total size of the container, minus the size of all volumes in the container.\n\nOn your computer, macOS is installed on a set of volumes known as a volume group. The volume group consists of one volume used for the system files (named Macintosh HD) and another volume used for data (named Macintosh HD - Data).\n\nOpen Disk Utility for me\n\n## Add an APFS volume\n\nEach volume you add to an APFS container can have a different APFS format, if needed.\n\n  1. Go to the Disk Utility app Image on your Mac.\n\nIf Disk Utility isn’t open, click Image: the Apps icon in the Dock, type Disk Utility in the Search field, then click Image: the Disk Utility icon.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMehrere macOS-Versionen auf dem Mac verwenden - Apple Support (DE) (https://support.apple.com/de-de/118282)\nciteturn1search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last week; Dieses „Dual Boot“-Setup ermöglicht es dir, gleichzeitig eine neuere Version von macOS auszuprobieren und dabei das aktuell installierte macOS für ältere Software zu behalten. ... Image: Dialogfenster des Assistenten zum Hinzufügen eines APFS-Volumes im Festplattendienstprogramm, über das ein neues APFS-Volume benannt und formatiert werden kann, um darauf ein zweites macOS zu installieren.\n\n# Mehrere macOS-Versionen auf dem Mac verwenden\n\nWenn du ein kompatibles macOS auf einem zusätzlichen internen oder externen Volume deines integrierten Startvolumes installierst, kann dein Mac von jedem der beiden Volumes aus gestartet werden.\n\n## Bevor du beginnst\n\nDieses „Dual Boot“-Setup ermöglicht es dir, gleichzeitig eine neuere Version von macOS auszuprobieren und dabei das aktuell installierte macOS für ältere Software zu behalten.\n\nWichtig: Dies ist keine langfristige Lösung, um eine ältere macOS-Version nach einem Upgrade weiterzuverwenden. Neuere macOS-Versionen könnten Änderungen zum Schutz deines Mac installieren, und diese Änderungen wären möglicherweise nicht mehr mit einer deutlich älteren Version deines Betriebssystems kompatibel.\n\n## Ein APFS-Volume für das neue macOS hinzufügen\n\nWenn du über genügend freien Speicherplatz verfügst, kannst du das neue macOS auf deinem eingebauten Startvolume installieren, ohne das bereits installierte macOS zu entfernen. Oder du installierst macOS auf einem externen Speichergerät.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInstall Windows 10 on your Mac with Boot Camp Assistant - Apple Support (https://support.apple.com/en-us/102622)\nciteturn1search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; With Boot Camp, you can install Microsoft Windows 10 on your Intel-based Mac, then switch between macOS and Windows when restarting your Mac. ... If you're using a Mac with the Apple T2 Security Chip, the default Secure Boot setting is Full Security.\n\n# Install Windows 10 on your Mac with Boot Camp Assistant\n\nWith Boot Camp, you can install Microsoft Windows 10 on your Intel-based Mac, then switch between macOS and Windows when restarting your Mac.\n\n## What you need to install Windows 10 on Mac\n\nBoot Camp requires one of these Mac models, which have an Intel processor:\n\n  * MacBook introduced in 2015 through 2017\n\n  * MacBook Air introduced in 2012 through 2020, excluding MacBook Air (M1, 2020)\n\n  * MacBook Pro introduced in 2012 through 2020, excluding MacBook Pro (13-inch, M1, 2020)\n\n  * Mac mini introduced in 2012 through 2018\n\n  * iMac introduced in 2012 through 2020^{1}\n\n  * iMac Pro (all models)\n\n  * Mac Pro introduced in 2013 through 2019\n\nThe latest macOS updates, which can include updates to Boot Camp Assistant. You will use Boot Camp Assistant to install Windows 10.\n\n64GB or more free storage space on your Mac st